  "live_records": {
    "q-94266": {
      "dom_id": "step-q94266",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ So, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{3}.$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm, we obtain the third column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
